     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Party</th>\n<th>Seat Size of 323</th>\n<th>Seat Size of 324</th>\n<th>Difference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Party A</td>\n<td>183</td>\n<td>184</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Party B</td>\n<td>124</td>\n<td>125</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Party C</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>–1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Party D</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Alabama paradox occurs when an increase in the house size reduces a state’s quota. Party C lost a seat despite an increase in the house size.</p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Yes, this demonstrates the Alabama paradox because State C receives 14 seats if the house size is 323, but only 13 seats if the house size is 324."
